CAT = "pllekPsych"; SEG = "PSY"
Q = [
('Which long-term effect of lithium needs regular monitoring?',
 'Hypothyroidism', ['Hyperthyroidism', 'Liver cirrhosis', 'Pulmonary fibrosis'],
 'Lithium causes hypothyroidism and reduces kidney function, so TSH, creatinine and calcium are checked regularly with the lithium level. Thiazides and NSAIDs raise levels.'),
('Which pair of findings points to neuroleptic malignant syndrome rather than serotonin syndrome?',
 'Lead-pipe rigidity, slow onset', ['Clonus, onset within hours', 'Mydriasis and diarrhoea', 'Hyperreflexia and shivering'],
 'NMS develops over days after a dopamine antagonist, with rigidity and high CK. Serotonin syndrome starts within hours, with clonus, hyperreflexia, mydriasis and diarrhoea.'),
('Serotonin syndrome persists despite stopping the drugs and giving benzodiazepines. Which antidote is used?',
 'Cyproheptadine', ['Dantrolene', 'Bromocriptine', 'Haloperidol'],
 'Cyproheptadine, a 5-HT2A antagonist, is used for moderate cases. Bromocriptine is a dopamine agonist used in NMS and can worsen serotonin toxicity.'),
('Which regular test is mandatory for a patient starting clozapine?',
 'Full blood count', ['Serum lithium level', 'Thyroid function tests', 'Liver biopsy'],
 'Clozapine can cause agranulocytosis, so the neutrophil count is checked weekly at first and regularly afterwards. It also needs watching for myocarditis and constipation.'),
('A heavy drinker on day 2 of admission is tremulous, sweating and hallucinating. First-line drug?',
 'Benzodiazepine', ['Haloperidol alone', 'Naltrexone', 'Disulfiram'],
 'Benzodiazepines such as diazepam or lorazepam treat alcohol withdrawal and prevent seizures and delirium tremens. Antipsychotics alone lower the seizure threshold.'),
('Wernicke encephalopathy classically combines confusion, ataxia and:',
 'Ophthalmoplegia', ['Hemiparesis', 'Expressive aphasia', 'Neck stiffness'],
 'The triad is confusion, ataxia and eye movement disorder (nystagmus or ophthalmoplegia). High-dose parenteral thiamine is given at once.'),
('Moderate depression 2 months after a myocardial infarction. Preferred antidepressant?',
 'Sertraline', ['Amitriptyline', 'Lithium', 'Diazepam'],
 'Sertraline has been shown to be safe after acute coronary syndromes. Tricyclics such as amitriptyline are arrhythmogenic and dangerous in overdose.'),
('Polish Mental Health Protection Act: admission without consent must be approved by the head of the ward within:',
 '48 hours', ['24 hours', '72 hours', '7 days'],
 'Under art. 23 of the act, the head of the ward must approve an admission without consent within 48 hours of it. Separately, the court must be told within 72 hours.'),
('After admitting a patient to a psychiatric hospital without consent, the hospital head notifies the guardianship court within:',
 '72 hours', ['24 hours', '10 days', '14 days'],
 'Art. 23 of the Mental Health Protection Act requires the head of the hospital to notify the guardianship court within 72 hours of the admission.'),
('Under Polish law, how long may admission without consent to clarify whether a person is mentally ill last?',
 'Up to 10 days', ['Up to 3 days', 'Up to 30 days', 'Up to 6 months'],
 'Art. 24 allows admission without consent for observation when behaviour threatens life or health and mental illness is uncertain, for no more than 10 days.'),
('On a psychiatric ward, who may order direct coercion when no doctor can decide immediately?',
 'Nurse, informing a doctor', ['Security guard on duty', "Patient's next of kin", 'Guardianship court'],
 "Art. 18 lets a nurse decide on and supervise direct coercion if a doctor's decision cannot be had at once; the nurse must tell the doctor without delay."),
('Mood stabiliser with proven anti-suicidal effect in bipolar disorder:',
 'Lithium', ['Valproate', 'Lamotrigine', 'Carbamazepine'],
 'Long-term lithium lowers suicide rates in mood disorders, an effect not shown for the anticonvulsant mood stabilisers. Valproate is also avoided in women who may become pregnant.'),
('Three days after starting feeding, a severely underweight patient with anorexia develops weakness and confusion. Which abnormality is typical?',
 'Hypophosphataemia', ['Hypernatraemia', 'Hypercalcaemia', 'Hyperkalaemia'],
 'Refeeding syndrome drives phosphate, potassium and magnesium into cells as insulin rises. Feeding starts low and increases slowly, with phosphate and thiamine supplements.'),
('An 80-year-old becomes acutely confused with fluctuating attention on day 2 after a hip operation. Likely diagnosis?',
 'Delirium', ['Alzheimer disease', 'Depressive episode', 'Frontotemporal dementia'],
 'Acute onset, fluctuating course and inattention define delirium. The cause, such as infection, pain, drugs or urinary retention, is found and treated.'),
('Which is a Schneiderian first-rank symptom of schizophrenia?',
 'Thought broadcasting', ['Flight of ideas', 'Psychomotor slowing', 'Circumstantial speech'],
 'First-rank symptoms include thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcasting, third-person running commentary and delusions of control. Flight of ideas is typical of mania.'),
('Recurrent panic attacks with avoidance for a year. Recommended long-term treatment?',
 'SSRI with CBT', ['Long-term alprazolam', 'Depot antipsychotic', 'Regular propranolol'],
 'An SSRI and cognitive behavioural therapy are first line for panic disorder. Long-term benzodiazepines cause dependence and are avoided.'),
]
